NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:08.780
There's been a lot of hype about neural networks and perhaps some of that hype is justified

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:10.740
given how well they're working.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:15.380
But it turns out that so far almost all the economic value created by neural networks

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:19.660
has been through one type of machine learning called supervised learning.

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:22.060
Let's see what that means and let's go over some examples.

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:27.540
In supervised learning, you have some input x and you want to learn a function mapping

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:29.980
out to some output y.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:34.540
So for example, just now we saw the housing price prediction application

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:40.620
where you input some features of a home and try to output or estimate the price y.

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:46.740
Here are some other examples that neural networks have been applied to very effectively.

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:52.620
Possibly the single most lucrative application of deep learning today is online advertising,

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:58.020
maybe not the most inspiring but certainly very lucrative in which by inputting

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:02.020
and add information about an ad to the website,

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:05.820
this thinking of showing you and some information about the user,

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:10.420
neural networks have gotten very good at predicting whether or not you click on an ad

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:15.100
and by showing you and showing users the ads that you're most likely to click on,

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:20.620
this has been an incredibly lucrative application of neural networks at multiple companies

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:24.460
because the ability to show you ads that you're more likely to click on

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:30.380
has a direct impact on the bottom line of some of the very large online advertising companies.

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:36.820
Computer vision has also made huge strides in the last several years, mostly due to deep learning.

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:42.540
So you might input an image and want to output an index, say from one to a thousand,

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:48.140
trying to tell you if this picture might be any one of, say, a thousand different images.

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:50.260
So you might use that for photo tagging.

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:54.380
I think the recent progress in speech recognition has also been very exciting,

00:01:54.380 --> 00:02:00.620
where you can now input an audio clip to a neural network and have it output a text transcript.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:04.700
Machine translation has also made huge strides thanks to deep learning,

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:10.660
where now you can have a neural network input an English sentence and directly output, say, a Chinese sentence.

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:14.260
And in autonomous driving, you might input an image,

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:20.100
say a picture of what's in front of your car, as well as some information from a radar.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:25.940
And based on that, maybe a neural network can be trained to tell you the position of the other cars on the road.

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:30.580
So this becomes a key component in autonomous driving systems.

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:37.660
So a lot of the value creation through neural networks has been through cleverly selecting what should be X

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:43.740
and what should be Y for your particular problem and then fitting this supervised learning component

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:48.300
into often a bigger system, such as an autonomous vehicle.

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:54.620
It turns out that slightly different types of neural networks are useful for different applications.

00:02:54.620 --> 00:03:00.540
For example, in the real estate application that we saw in the previous video,

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:04.060
we used a relatively standard neural network architecture.

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:10.100
And maybe for real estate and online advertising, it might be a relatively standard neural network,

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:13.100
like the one that we saw.

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:21.340
For image applications, we'll often use convolutional neural networks, often abbreviated CNN.

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:27.540
And for sequence data, so for example, audio has a temporal component, right?

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:29.300
Audio is played out over time.

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:35.460
So audio is most naturally represented as a one dimensional time series.

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:37.740
It's a one dimensional temporal sequence.

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:44.900
And so for sequence data, you often use an RNN, a recurrent neural network.

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:50.060
Language, you know, English and Chinese, the alphabets or the words come one at a time.

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:54.700
So language is also most naturally represented as sequence data.

00:03:54.700 --> 00:04:00.380
And so more complex versions of RNNs are often used for these applications.

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:05.060
And then for more complex applications like autonomous driving, where you have an image

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:09.940
that might suggest more of a CNN convolutional neural net structure and radar info, which

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:17.700
is something quite different, you might end up with a more custom or some more complex

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:20.820
hybrid neural network architecture.

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:27.900
So just a bit more concrete about what are the standard CNN and RNN architectures.

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:32.740
So in the literature, you might have seen pictures like this.

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:34.500
So that's a standard neural net.

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:36.500
You might have seen pictures like this.

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:40.300
Well, this is an example of a convolutional neural network.

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:45.420
And we'll see in a later course exactly what this picture means and how you can implement

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:46.420
this.

00:04:46.420 --> 00:04:51.460
But convolutional networks are often used for image data.

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:53.940
And you might also have seen pictures like this.

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:59.260
And you learn how to implement this in a later course where current neural networks are

00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:06.060
very good for this type of one dimensional sequence data that is maybe a temporal component.

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:11.740
You might also have heard about applications of machine learning to both structured data

00:05:11.740 --> 00:05:13.860
and unstructured data.

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:15.100
Here's what the terms mean.

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:19.820
Structured data means basically databases of data.

00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:25.900
So for example, in housing price prediction, you might have a database with a column that

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:28.020
tells you the size and the number of bedrooms.

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:29.980
So this is structured data.

00:05:29.980 --> 00:05:35.540
Or in predicting whether or not a user will click on an ad, you might have information

00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:40.180
about the user, such as the age, some information on the ad, and then labels why that you're

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:41.500
trying to predict.

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:48.380
So that's structured data, meaning that each of the features, such as size of a house,

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:54.420
a number of bedrooms, or the age of a user has a very well-defined meaning.

00:05:54.420 --> 00:06:01.620
In contrast, unstructured data refers to things like audio, raw audio, or images where you

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:05.620
might want to recognize what's in the image, or text.

00:06:05.620 --> 00:06:10.860
Here the features might be the pixel values in an image or the individual words in a piece

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:11.860
of text.

00:06:11.860 --> 00:06:17.020
Historically, it's been much harder for computers to make sense of unstructured data

00:06:17.020 --> 00:06:19.380
compared to structured data.

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:24.940
And in fact, the human race has evolved to be very good at understanding audio cues as

00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:25.940
well as images.

00:06:25.940 --> 00:06:30.220
And then text was a more recent invention, but people are just really good at interpreting

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:31.700
unstructured data.

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:36.860
And so one of the most exciting things about the rise of neural networks is that thanks

00:06:36.860 --> 00:06:41.940
to deep learning, thanks to neural networks, computers are now much better at interpreting

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:46.220
unstructured data as well compared to just a few years ago.

00:06:46.220 --> 00:06:52.300
And this creates opportunities for many new exciting applications that use speech recognition,

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:58.100
image recognition, natural language processing on text, much more than was possible even

00:06:58.100 --> 00:06:59.860
just two or three years ago.

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:04.860
I think because people have a natural empathy to understanding unstructured data, you might

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:09.980
hear about neural network successes on unstructured data more in the media because it's just

00:07:09.980 --> 00:07:12.860
cool when a neural network recognizes a cat.

00:07:12.860 --> 00:07:15.620
We all like that and we all know what that means.

00:07:15.620 --> 00:07:19.540
But it turns out that a lot of the short term economic value that neural networks are

00:07:19.540 --> 00:07:26.860
creating has also been on structured data such as much better advertising systems, much better

00:07:26.860 --> 00:07:33.460
product recommendations and just a much better ability to process the giant databases that

00:07:33.460 --> 00:07:37.180
many companies have to make accurate predictions from them.

00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:41.900
So in this course, a lot of the techniques we'll go over will apply to both structured

00:07:41.900 --> 00:07:44.580
data and to unstructured data.

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:49.820
For the purposes of explaining the algorithms, we will draw a little bit more on examples

00:07:49.820 --> 00:07:52.020
that use unstructured data.

00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:56.940
But as you think through applications of neural networks within your own team, I hope you find

00:07:56.940 --> 00:08:02.540
both uses for them in both structured and unstructured data.

00:08:02.540 --> 00:08:07.980
So neural networks have transformed supervised learning and are creating tremendous economic

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:09.380
value.

00:08:09.380 --> 00:08:13.500
It turns out though that the basic technical ideas behind neural networks have mostly been

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:16.420
around sometimes for many decades.

00:08:16.420 --> 00:08:20.940
So why is it then that they're only just now taking off and working so well?

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:25.620
In the next video, we'll talk about why it's only quite recently that neural networks

00:08:25.620 --> 00:08:28.740
have become this incredibly powerful tool that you can use.
